    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 283,
        "text": "Welcome, everyone. Today we look at three claims about productivity. First, a company says most workers are more productive at home. Second, a blog claims all people work better with music. Third, a report suggests some meetings are a waste of time. Let's examine the evidence.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "927a643640867a2d525221625a2d498373512fc709b40200c4ee81a9406f7a69",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 290,
        "sourceEnd": 469,
        "text": "I read the company study. It states that 60 percent of its staff finished tasks faster at home. But the sample was only 30 people. So we should say the study suggests, not proves.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "2fa03f3296129944a395242cff1e6c4b96761528b00fb414627a6b3b27997b57",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 477,
        "sourceEnd": 670,
        "text": "Good point. The blog says all people work better with music. But my sister, for example, finds music distracting. So the word 'all' is too strong. We can say some people work better with music.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "26f7df3bf24009bade099dbbb0671ba239c341f6dfbcb3512de92eade49818c3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 677,
        "sourceEnd": 812,
        "text": "Exactly. The report on meetings says some meetings are a waste of time. That seems reasonable. It doesn't say all meetings are useless.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "2e278799798bf39a3f3904604612066d1e9e3ca498ecf0a746654fae943db2de",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 820,
        "sourceEnd": 1011,
        "text": "Right. Now, how do we report these claims? We can use verbs like 'states', 'claims', 'suggests', and 'argues'. For example, 'The company claims that most workers are more productive at home.'",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "be25ae08cf815957a3773adfde44526aa9ecac7fde06914084553561550a36ea",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 1018,
        "sourceEnd": 1141,
        "text": "And we can paraphrase: 'According to the company, many employees work faster at home.' That avoids copying the exact words.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "142f3fc901e305069f8f4fe82ca06f563048730c4b36b72633fade392e80caf5",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1149,
        "sourceEnd": 1375,
        "text": "Yes. Also, be careful with quantifiers. 'All' means 100 percent. 'Most' means more than half but not all. 'Some' means a few, not many. So 'some meetings are a waste of time' is weaker than 'most meetings are a waste of time'.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "4d1959ae36c2f2da3fc2c94582b8d8be6618bd2fc5c9d42cd5d10b34b91a2f41",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 1382,
        "sourceEnd": 1509,
        "text": "I see. So when we report, we should keep the original quantifier. If the original says 'some', we shouldn't change it to 'all'.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "89646a9740c86eef1b8b5a1781f5d13ff86ee59fdb68a98764651c6c1f824eed",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1517,
        "sourceEnd": 1624,
        "text": "Exactly. Now, let's practice. I'll give you a claim, and you report it with a suitable verb and quantifier.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "498aedb22544031a07238a5f434f18bb851f2c799a63a5566474580aafed50fd",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 1631,
        "sourceEnd": 1766,
        "text": "Okay. The claim: 'Many employees prefer flexible hours.' I can report: 'The survey suggests that many employees prefer flexible hours.'",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "578691039edeb8c392d3fef535ba71bd6f76bd95a6dd7339edc8f2dd68d7a191",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1774,
        "sourceEnd": 1916,
        "text": "Good. Remember to use 'that' after reporting verbs when you report a full clause. And for paraphrasing, change the words but keep the meaning.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "03a0b32b3160e3fbacc1d782688fa5f3abfa70245a5cf83191c4403d032fb371",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 1923,
        "sourceEnd": 1998,
        "text": "Got it. So the key is to be accurate with both the verb and the quantifier.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "4b330ea98bd6bf1700d5b7c55934a52b0482c452dbef7fdeb6edddd9f213eb1d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2006,
        "sourceEnd": 2043,
        "text": "Yes. Let's move on to the next claim.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "a6dfeb2a0a87cff16cf79ce2679f32f806313cff8cca9cb3aae99ca26747c236",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
